values ('b6c7d8e9-0f1a-4b41-8e01-1c2d3e4f5a41', 'scriptural-languages-hebrew-greek', 'Scriptural Languages (Hebrew/Greek)', 'Students learn why Hebrew and Greek matter for scriptural interpretation and how language study changes reading, translation, theology, history, and ethics. The course builds Biblical Hebrew and Koine Greek reading fluency, then develops grammar habits, vocabulary methods, nominal and verbal systems, and genre-sensitive syntax. Students treat translation as disciplined interpretation, use lexicons, grammars, parsers, concordances, corpora, and databases critically, and learn how textual witnesses and apparatus entries affect translation without reducing scripture to uncertainty. Close reading and a translation commentary connect grammar to rhetoric, genre, theology, historical context, and responsible public communication.');

