5d-5749-4e9b-856e-4210aaefc0d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Nominative, genitive, dative, accusative, vocative"}, {"id": "1c6072ef-cdf2-4544-9822-db86d71de567", "type": "concept", "title": "First, second, and third declensions"}, {"id": "dfc218f2-3709-48f9-a0b7-ee5a475e6360", "type": "concept", "title": "Case functions and translation choices"}], "description": "Students learn how Greek marks grammatical relationships."}, {"id": "1c402c2e-10c9-43e6-972e-3c98e7926efb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Articles, adjectives, and pronouns", "children": [{"id": "4517133d-cb5c-4cf9-a6d3-33297e0b0554", "type": "concept", "title": "Article usage, substantival adjectives, attributive/predicate positions, and agreement"}, {"id": "f5d4bb28-6982-429a-920e-074551375e1d", "type": "concept", "title": "Personal, demonstrative, relative, interrogative, indefinite, and reflexive pronouns"}, {"id": "543bedaa-8b79-468e-9d87-033d1c2c78c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Granville Sharp and other advanced article issues as appropriate"}], "description": "The gist is to interpret Greek noun phrases accurately."}, {"id": "8bd20795-812f-44dc-ad9e-86476e785b2a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Participles and prepositions", "children": [{"id": "658312aa-8fbf-49eb-87d9-977241367ec8", "type": "concept", "title": "Adjectival, substantival, adverbial, genitive absolute, and periphrastic participles"}, {"id": "3130901b-2f79-4260-b90f-c88158849408", "type": "concept", "title": "Prepositional phrases and case meanings"}, {"id": "a0c72fdf-d78c-49ba-891d-7d8d50ee9947", "type": "concept", "title": "Temporal, causal, concessive, conditional, and means relationships"}], "description": "Students learn structures that often carry compressed meaning."}]}, {"id": "4a673a04-4515-457a-a27d-1410c47eaf9f", "type": "topic", "title": "Greek Verbal System: Tense, Aspect, Voice, Mood, Participles, Infinitives, and Principal Parts", "description": "Students learn how Greek verbs communicate action, viewpoint, agency, condition, command, and discourse movement.", "children": [{"id": "46220f0a-d326-4362-8c31-25b1159dc7c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Tense and aspect", "children": [{"id": "383b0156-99ba-4936-9843-d5d0816e3dbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Present, imperfect, aorist, perfect, pluperfect, future"}, {"id": "b6d4e89b-4826-4d9f-a86a-5132128dd016", "type": "concept", "title": "Imperfective, perfective, and stative aspect"}, {"id": "a0318ec1-099c-4404-81b2-6a8fd7338337", "type": "concept", "title": "Aktionsart, context, and discourse function"}], "description": "Students learn to interpret Greek verbs without over-translating tense labels."}, {"id": "97ff4f7b-90b9-44de-ad05-3555dfac2d51", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voice and mood", "children": [{"id": "1b961041-a992-4ddf-afce-4f4dfb3ee889", "type": "concept", "title": "Active, middle, passive, deponent, and mediopassive forms"}, {"id": "d78a3430-b4a2-4e4a-b49c-3d11331cc811", "type": "concept", "title": "Indicative, subjunctive, optative, imperative"}, {"id": "6f827476-cb1b-4ef2-90db-cced310417bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Conditionals, prohibitions, purpose, result, and exhortation"}], "description": "The point is to understand agency, possibility, command, and condition."}, {"id": "9b3c6147-729f-412d-8cfb-3b4ade4902bb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Nonfinite forms", "children": [{"id": "39ad4615-c52a-4451-bc13-b04141b94f55", "type": "concept", "title": "Participles, infinitives, complementary infinitives, articular infinitives, and indirect discourse"}, {"id": "1b154f53-69f2-495d-9afd-84cc7c0d2cbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Principal parts and stem recognition"}, {"id": "2d34e2d8-0a89-4bf6-b725-59beb54524da", "type": "concept", "title": "Complex verbal chains and translation strategy"}], "description": "Students master forms that drive Greek syntax."}]}, {"id": "a0d31153-f29d-4035-b48a-a8faff7fedc1", "type": "topic", "title": "Syntax and Clause Structure: Hebrew Narrative/Poetry and Greek Discourse", "description": "This module moves from forms to sentences, helping students interpret clauses, word order, conjunctions, discourse markers, and genre-specific syntax.", "children": [{"id": "df9d844e-f55b-4a3d-9b1f-f37ecd8a9952", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hebrew syntax", "children": [{"id": "0126a5d2-7a65-49f6-b67b-ffeae44a61ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Word order, waw, verbless clauses, subordinate clauses, and discourse sequencing"}, {"id": "926f90b9-753b-435f-a115-f56d2dfe1bf4", "type": "concept", "title": "Parallelism, ellipsis, chiasm, and poetic lines"}, {"id": "f0160be6-3c6b-4b92-9dcb-4627f2dc44fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative pace, foreground/background, and direct speech"}], "description": "Students learn how Hebrew clauses create narrative, emphasis, and poetry."}, {"id": "80351d11-112b-478c-adc5-95550d7b28dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Greek syntax", "children": [{"id": "e0fd7651-3cba-4c33-a2de-b1bf2ff4db34", "type": "concept", "title": "Main/subordinate clauses, conjunctions, particles, relative clauses, and indirect discourse"}, {"id": "8d1ff682-8d1d-4524-b242-4e4c5dda111d", "type": "concept", "title": "Discourse markers, topic/focus, word order, and emphasis"}, {"id": "a636d575-d7a0-4979-92e6-91a68a9dfcfe", "type": "concept", "title": "Long sentences and periodic structure"}], "description": "The gist is to move from isolated forms to argument flow."}, {"id": "90f23ccb-7b66-4c83-9f72-d55edf53d841", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genre-sensitive reading", "children": [{"id": "a5dc31d8-81d0-47ba-8088-72c9518aa224", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative, law, prophecy, wisdom, psalm, gospel, parable, epistle, and apocalypse"}, {"id": "6ec4e86d-2d20-487c-b880-2ebdac4d9c85", "type": "concept", "title": "Syntax and rhetorical effect"}, {"id": "4e7296e4-bb71-4c68-b446-2cd9c69fc79d", "type": "concept", "title": "Grammar as one layer of interpretation"}], "description": "Students adjust interpretation to literary context."}]}, {"id": "345507a7-6858-495f-89db-287ce58d7aa0", "type": "topic", "title": "Translation Method: Literal, Literary, Functional, Theological, and Annotated Translation", "description": "Students learn translation as disciplined interpretation, balancing accuracy, readability, genre, audience, and transparent annotation.", "children": [{"id": "df0a8815-a71f-4f2c-be6d-e397cdd89727", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Translation philosophies", "children": [{"id": "cea2f592-5556-453b-9ed8-6c12074d0689", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal equivalence, functional equivalence, literary translation, and paraphrase"}, {"id": "5564fa5e-a407-4300-a2a9-031f599ec724", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, genre, register, and readability"}, {"id": "ee1439b4-b895-4f13-bbdb-aab125cc0d1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation committees and theological stakes"}], "description": "Students learn why translations differ."}, {"id": "fde73c6c-a90d-407d-b646-f2e779b4af2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decision-making process", "children": [{"id": "9e034df7-98ce-4ace-a352-0bf86626ebcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Parsing, syntax, lexical range, context, genre, and intertextuality"}, {"id": "f6bc870c-5dff-4383-a61a-3be9211faf14", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, idiom, metaphor, and culturally embedded terms"}, {"id": "0fe4030b-b8d7-464e-bad2-0d682f0a08ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Footnotes, brackets, and explanatory comments"}], "description": "The point is to make translation choices visible and defensible."}, {"id": "a70b7ce6-2aeb-4df9-92bf-45eda6b9621a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparing translations", "children": [{"id": "caecc4e2-c5bc-4715-bbba-a54832a28dcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Major English translations and their philosophies"}, {"id": "729446ad-9842-400e-840f-99d9db87e0b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Hebrew Bible, Septuagint, and New Testament comparison"}, {"id": "2f7def58-fcce-42b1-838e-d1759bb7a02a", "type": "concept", "title": "Detecting interpretive decisions in wording"}], "description": "Students use published translations as evidence, not shortcuts."}]}, {"id": "96d6c488-2b2a-4653-a2b7-c37b30c5f450", "type": "topic", "title": "Lexicons, Grammars, Concordances, Corpora, and Digital Language Tools", "description": "This topic trains students to use reference tools critically rather than mechanically, including lexicons, grammars, parsers, concordances, corpora, and databases.", "children": [{"id": "37e96990-65a7-4b93-ae4b-81460ca5c847", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lexicon use", "children": [{"id": "1ce3ba2b-f13d-492d-96fe-c4c583603f2b", "type": "concept", "title": "HALOT, BDB, BDAG, LSJ, glosses, entries, citations, and semantic domains"}, {"id": "1d5b41da-53c6-4830-be71-0ef7bc7f935e", "type": "concept", "title": "Root fallacies, etymological fallacies, and selective evidence"}, {"id": "dcd4f542-d3cb-49b3-80c2-e7cbda9bc4a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Choosing a meaning from usage and context"}], "description": "Students learn to use dictionaries responsibly."}, {"id": "5cbd2890-0808-4788-af6b-28f569008a96", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Grammar and concordance workflow", "children": [{"id": "3c930fb1-3d52-4638-a038-06045b10a2bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Reference grammars, syntax guides, concordances, cross-references, and indices"}, {"id": "0c4a3457-a389-45b3-8978-c3326184ffc5", "type": "concept", "title": "When to consult a grammar versus a lexicon"}, {"id": "7bdd93c7-d3c8-42e9-9edc-478ec8b13dfa", "type": "concept", "title": "Documenting evidence in notes"}], "description": "The gist is to build a reliable reference process."}, {"id": "a9381923-f176-4335-90ef-32a41885600a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital tools", "children": [{"id": "15be41d3-46e5-4d27-9a4f-8d42c5324c14", "type": "concept", "title": "Sefaria, STEP Bible, Blue Letter Bible, Bible software, corpora, and morphology databases"}, {"id": "e79e87c8-70d4-47de-8a55-a0d862f31b76", "type": "concept", "title": "Parsers, tagged texts, searches, and exportable notes"}, {"id": "b6d39506-8cfb-4896-bebf-7e46238641c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Verification, bias, licensing, and citation"}], "description": "Students learn to benefit from software while retaining language judgment."}]}, {"id": "cae8ff16-083d-4ae0-8411-ccf1b528fc37", "type": "topic", "title": "Textual Criticism, Manuscripts, Variants, Apparatus, Masoretic Text, Septuagint, and New Testament Witnesses", "description": "Students learn how textual witnesses and apparatus entries affect translation and interpretation without reducing scripture to uncertainty.", "children": [{"id": "2d6f10ae-63d5-429b-945a-f81c2f9eeb14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Manuscript traditions", "children": [{"id": "3007689a-c8d6-450b-9601-f3a891c7bbdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Masoretic Text, Dead Sea Scrolls, Samaritan Pentateuch, Septuagint, Vulgate, Peshitta, and Targums"}, {"id": "f02c1198-8c53-47e6-8581-ed40db4adf21", "type": "concept", "title": "Papyri, uncials, minuscules, lectionaries, and patristic citations"}, {"id": "f5cd71b8-cc9a-4c26-9d9f-abb608100260", "type": "concept", "title": "Transmission, scribes, and communities"}], "description": "Students learn where printed texts come from."}, {"id": "e5a9e95c-9567-4fdc-9402-f370812430f1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Apparatus skills", "children": [{"id": "08a6ece0-5e9c-417e-8a68-cb4e91286325", "type": "concept", "title": "Sigla, witnesses, variants, conjectures, omissions, additions, and orthographic differences"}, {"id": "bd81db3a-277c-41cd-ace8-1953983cdcd0", "type": "concept", "title": "BHS/BHQ apparatus and Nestle-Aland apparatus"}, {"id": "63de6d07-3374-43de-b6ea-960de717dda6", "type": "concept", "title": "External and internal evidence"}], "description": "The point is to read critical notes without panic."}, {"id": "92566488-4a0b-4580-9309-4b8a48edd5bd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive impact", "children": [{"id": "198d1e2b-9942-4471-83b6-7ed2efe76598", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaningful and viable variants"}, {"id": "cda88ab9-84bb-454b-9268-70d68a6f2987", "type": "concept", "title": "Textual decisions and translation notes"}, {"id": "338a7fe1-4fdd-46ac-8d8b-364651b4b9dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical communication of uncertainty in teaching and public writing"}], "description": "Students evaluate when variants matter."}]}, {"id": "f94599d0-2fb8-462c-87a6-60acb988dd08", "type": "topic", "title": "Close Reading Workshops: Hebrew Bible, Septuagint, New Testament, Psalms, Prophets, Gospels, and Epistles", "description": "Students practice slow reading of selected passages in Hebrew and Greek, connecting grammar to rhetoric, genre, theology, and historical context.", "children": [{"id": "34916b73-b76d-47b3-8fad-2a11aae62b6a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Hebrew Bible readings", "children": [{"id": "fba9ae38-7fa6-4bd0-a57c-dc989891ae71", "type": "concept", "title": "Genesis narrative, Exodus law, Psalms, Isaiah or another prophet, and wisdom texts"}, {"id": "83d2354a-6ac3-4fde-b611-989d704620e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Narrative verbs, construct chains, parallelism, and poetic compression"}, {"id": "71ed6ff8-0823-4fc3-8fb2-fdb628b78926", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation and theological interpretation"}], "description": "Students practice reading Hebrew passages with grammatical and literary attention."}, {"id": "82eb4b95-676e-42ac-89ba-3d9081a55652", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Greek readings", "children": [{"id": "49eb1e2c-7f94-4fec-81a9-c1f7072dd907", "type": "concept", "title": "Gospel narrative, Pauline argument, general epistle, and Septuagint passage"}, {"id": "45500bf1-56b7-4545-9668-f7b9c287a8ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Participles, infinitives, case usage, conjunctions, and discourse flow"}, {"id": "c03ef4ee-fd53-4433-869d-11ea215fbe91", "type": "concept", "title": "Syntax, rhetoric, and interpretation"}], "description": "The gist is to apply Greek grammar to real Koine texts."}, {"id": "26d77f5e-5191-49e0-9006-429da2fc584e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative workshops", "children": [{"id": "5513d399-9047-4980-a2af-62d0e2f7959d", "type": "concept", "title": "Hebrew Bible and Septuagint differences"}, {"id": "505f47df-10c4-4df6-8a72-489e4f03fd4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Old Testament citations in the New Testament"}, {"id": "594e49ba-e4bd-4b7e-96b2-9fd595d24bfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Intertextuality, reception, and interpretive history"}], "description": "Students compare Hebrew, Greek, and translation traditions where relevant."}]}, {"id": "841ed8a1-9949-4a16-abab-5561a4364ce3", "type": "topic", "title": "Translation Commentary Capstone: Exegesis, Ethics, Teaching, and Public Interpretation", "description": "Students synthesize the course by producing a translation commentary that explains language decisions, textual issues, interpretive stakes, and responsible communication.", "children": [{"id": "91f7c97f-2b86-4daa-b6cd-90d779f3e8e6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project design", "children": [{"id": "8bb5a842-6dba-4bd7-bfa6-ddaeacda6973", "type": "concept", "title": "Passage selection, genre, textual witnesses, and bibliography"}, {"id": "113ac9b7-6fff-48b4-93b2-4e31f0547527", "type": "concept", "title": "Hebrew or Greek focus, with optional comparison"}, {"id": "23341521-7f09-4563-b1ad-313e59af4b4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope, thesis, and research plan"}], "description": "Students choose a passage and define a manageable research question."}, {"id": "ef9c6666-a4af-4c03-bcf1-c4ea8280ac04", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Commentary writing", "children": [{"id": "e9f2dc25-50c8-4492-963e-e0fbd7977534", "type": "concept", "title": "Original-language text, parsing notes, lexical decisions, syntax, variants, and translation"}, {"id": "502a91b0-8e3a-487e-bdc2-dc2ef5b70bb9", "type": "concept", "title": "Engagement with grammars, lexicons, commentaries, and scholarly articles"}, {"id": "9513a059-b5c5-48ff-96fb-e57b4d246170", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument, evidence, and citation"}], "description": "The point is to integrate language evidence into clear interpretation."}, {"id": "841280ed-632e-4ce5-9af1-06a839ee5b66", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public communication", "children": [{"id": "6bf309a0-f5ec-4cf3-a195-c5895c403109", "type": "concept", "title": "Teaching handout, study note, sermon-prep brief, museum/archives label, or public essay"}, {"id": "df31a66a-f7a4-4206-a739-6bb2151c079f", "type": "concept", "title": "Explaining uncertainty and disagreement responsibly"}, {"id": "ad2c5c5b-14f6-4fc2-b93d-ad6d617aa075", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection on ethics, audience, and interpretive humility"}], "description": "Students learn to translate technical work for non-specialists."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$scrijson$::jsonb,
